{"ambient_context_auto_assumed": false, "available_prior_named_context": [{"kind": "definition", "label": "definition-formally-smooth", "line": 3081}], "candidate_id": "candidate.stacks.3808b3ec2ad3539482a2ad5e", "candidate_review_status": "needs_actual_mathematical_standalone_reconstruction", "context_events_since_previous_candidate": [{"environment": "definition", "kind": "enter_local_environment", "line": 3081, "persists_after_end": false, "scope_stack": ["definition"]}, {"environment": "definition", "kind": "exit_local_environment", "line": 3093, "persists_after_end": false, "scope_stack_before_pop": ["definition"]}, {"environment": "lemma", "kind": "enter_local_environment", "line": 3110, "persists_after_end": false, "scope_stack": ["lemma"]}], "explicit_refs": [], "new_refs_in_this_candidate": [], "prior_conclusions": ["A composition of formally smooth morphisms is formally smooth."], "prior_context_status": "resolved", "prior_hypotheses": ["A composition of formally smooth morphisms"], "proof_screened": false, "provisional_durable_scope_assertions": [], "schema": "maththm.section-walk-candidate.v1", "section_label": "section-formally-smooth", "section_opening_preview": "Michael Artin's position on differential criteria of smoothness (e.g.,\nMorphisms, Lemma \\ref{morphisms-lemma-smooth-at-point}) is that they are\nbasically useless (in practice). In this section we introduce the\nnotion of a formally smooth morphism $X \\to S$. Such a morphism is\ncharacterized by the property that $T$-valued points of $X$ lift\nto infinitesimal thickenings of $T$ provided $T$ is affine.\nThe main result is that a morphism which is formally smooth and\nlocally of finite presentation is smooth, see\nLemma \\ref{lemma-smooth-formally-smooth}.\nIt turns out that this criterion is often easier to use than the\ndifferential criteria mentioned above.\nRecall that a ring map $R \\to A$ is called {\\it formally smooth}\n(see Algebra, Definition \\ref{algebra-definition-formally-smooth})\nif for every commutative solid diagram\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\nA \\ar[r] \\ar@{-->}[rd] & B/I \\\\\nR \\ar[r] \\ar[u] & B \\ar[u]\n}\n$$\nwhere $I \\subset B$ is an ideal of square zero, a dotted\narrow exists which makes the diagram commute. This motivates\nthe following analogue for morphisms of schemes.", "section_start_line": 3051, "section_title": "Formally smooth morphisms", "source_filename": "more-morphisms.tex", "source_label": "lemma-composition-formally-smooth", "source_revision": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_statement_line": 3110, "source_url": "https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-morphisms.tex#L3110", "statement_tex": "\\label{lemma-composition-formally-smooth}\nA composition of formally smooth morphisms is formally smooth.", "statement_truncated": false}
